meta {
  name: 01 Register
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/center-auth/register
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "centerName": "Goethe Language Center",
    "managerFirstName": "Alain",
    "managerLastName": "Ngeukeu",
    "email": "{{centerEmail}}",
    "password": "{{centerPassword}}"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
  res.body.message: eq verification email sent
}

docs {
  Set `centerEmail` and `centerPassword` in the environment first. Use a fresh
  address each run, or step 02 will fail — a verified account cannot re-verify.

  ## Five fields, and only five

  Registration takes the center name, the manager's name, an email and a
  password. Nothing else. A center registers, verifies, and lands on a
  dashboard; country, city, phone and logo arrive later during onboarding,
  because none of them is needed to run a trial.

  Each of these must return 400 VALIDATION_ERROR, not a 201:

  - `"country": "Cameroon"`
  - `"city": "Douala"`
  - `"phone": "+237690000000"`
  - `"logoUrl": "https://cdn.example.com/logo.webp"`

  They are REFUSED, not ignored. A client still sending the old shape has to
  hear about it rather than have its data silently dropped — which is exactly
  what would have happened if the fields were merely unused.

  ## What one registration creates

  Four rows, in one transaction: the center, its owner, its subscription, and
  its single trial seat — `center_seats(START, 1, 0)`. A trial is an ordinary
  seat row priced at zero rather than a flag, so nothing downstream needs an
  "unless they are on trial" branch. Check it with
  `Center Subscription / 02 Get usage`: `seatsLimit` is 1 and `perTier` has one
  START entry.

  If registration fails, none of the four exists. Re-run with the same address
  and count `center_seats` — it must not grow.

  ## The anti-enumeration property

  Re-run this request with the same address: it must return the identical 201
  body, never a conflict. A different answer here tells an attacker which
  addresses are registered, so it is a defect rather than a nicety.

  The verification token is emailed, not returned. Copy it from the server
  console into the `verificationToken` variable before running step 02.
}
